.TH NETMANAGER_BLUE 1 "2026-11-08" "holyc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netmanager_blue \- small network and bluetooth manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B netmanager_blue
[\fBstatus\fR]
.br
.B netmanager_blue wifi
list | connect \fISSID\fR [\fIPASSPHRASE\fR] | disconnect | forget \fISSID\fR
.br
.B netmanager_blue wifi wps
pbc | pin [\fIPIN\fR] | cancel
.br
.B netmanager_blue wifi hotspot
start \fISSID\fR [\fIPASSPHRASE\fR] | stop
.br
.B netmanager_blue wifi known
[autoconnect \fISSID\fR on|off]
.br
.B netmanager_blue bt
list | scan [\fISEC\fR] | connect|disconnect|pair|trust|remove \fINAME_OR_MAC\fR | power on|off
.br
.B netmanager_blue bt agent
[\fISEC\fR]
.br
.B netmanager_blue bt pair
\fINAME_OR_MAC\fR \fB\-\-pin\fR \fIPIN\fR
.br
.B netmanager_blue fw
.SH DESCRIPTION
No libraries: it talks D\-Bus to iwd and bluez, or the control socket of wpa_supplicant, and reads sysfs.
.B status
shows ethernet, wifi and bluetooth. The wifi backend is chosen automatically: wpa_supplicant when
.I /run/wpa_supplicant/wlan0
exists (this needs root), otherwise iwd. The interface can be changed with NMB_IFACE.
.B fw
prints the nft ruleset (root).
.SH WIFI: WPS, HOTSPOT, SAVED NETWORKS
.B wifi wps pbc
starts WPS push\-button and waits (up to NMB_WPS_SECS, default 120) for the result: with wpa_supplicant it ATTACHes to the control socket, sends WPS_PBC and watches WPS\-SUCCESS / WPS\-FAIL / WPS\-TIMEOUT / WPS\-OVERLAP events; with iwd it calls SimpleConfiguration.PushButton.
.B wifi wps pin
[PIN] uses a 4 or 8 digit PIN; without one the router\-side PIN is generated (wpa_supplicant WPS_PIN any, iwd GeneratePin) and printed.
.B wifi wps cancel
aborts a running WPS.
.PP
.B wifi hotspot start
SSID [PASSPHRASE] switches the device to access\-point mode: iwd's AccessPoint interface (the passphrase is required, 8\-63 characters), or a wpa_supplicant network with mode=2, WPA2\-PSK/CCMP, on NMB_AP_FREQ (default 2437 MHz) and without a passphrase an open one. The network is tagged id_str=nmb\-ap so
.B hotspot stop
removes exactly that one (iwd: Stop and back to station mode). It does not run DHCP or NAT; give the interface an address and share the uplink with dnsmasq/nft yourself.
.PP
.B wifi known
lists saved networks with their autoconnect state;
.B wifi known autoconnect
SSID on|off changes it (iwd KnownNetwork.AutoConnect, or wpa_supplicant ENABLE_NETWORK/DISABLE_NETWORK).
.PP
SSIDs and passphrases containing quotes, backslashes or newlines are refused under wpa_supplicant because they would break out of the control\-socket command.
.SH BLUETOOTH: PAIRING AGENT
.B bt pair
registers its own org.bluez.Agent1 as the default agent for the duration of the pairing and answers bluez on the terminal: PIN code, passkey (0\-999999), "does this passkey match? [y/N]", pairing and service authorisation, and it shows displayed PINs/passkeys. Input comes from stdin. With
.B \-\-pin
PIN nothing is asked: every PIN or passkey request is answered with PIN and every confirmation or authorisation is accepted, so use it only for devices you are pairing on purpose.
.B bt agent
[SEC] only registers the agent and serves requests (for pairing started from the phone) until SEC seconds have passed, or Ctrl\-C when SEC is omitted.
.SH LIBRARY
The logic lives in the stb-style single header netmanager_blue.h (define NMB_IMPLEMENTATION in one .c file; functions nmb_status, nmb_wifi_*, nmb_bt_*, nmb_fw; output redirectable via nmb_out/nmb_err, prompt answers via nmb_in). This program is a thin CLI over it.
.SH NOT YET
Ethernet configuration, a TUI; wifi forget works with iwd only. WPS, hotspot and the pairing agent are tested against fake wpa_supplicant / iwd / bluez peers (tests/netmanager_blue_test.c), not yet against real hardware.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iwctl (1),
.BR bluetoothctl (1),
.BR nft (8)
.SH VPN
Header nmb_vpn.h (define NMB_VPN_IMPLEMENTATION; functions nmbv_*; front end nmbv_cmd). Profiles live in /etc/netmanager_blue/vpn/NAME.conf (override with NMBV_CONF_DIR); a file with [Interface] is WireGuard, anything else (or NAME.ovpn) is OpenVPN. Profiles must be owned by root (or the caller) and not group/world writable, because PostUp/PreDown hooks run as shell commands, as in wg-quick. State lives in /run/netmanager_blue/vpn (NMBV_RUN_DIR).
.TP
.B nmbv list | up NAME | down NAME | status NAME | logs NAME
WireGuard is native: link, address, MTU, peers, fwmark, routes and the wg-quick policy rules are set over netlink, no wg(8) or wg-quick. DNS from the profile is swapped into resolv.conf and restored on down. OpenVPN runs as openvpn --daemon with a pidfile and a log; the auth-user-pass file must be mode 0600 and credentials never appear on argv.
.TP
.B nmbv ks NAME on|off
nftables kill switch (table inet nmbv_ks, replaced atomically, survives tunnel flaps). Only the tunnel, loopback, the endpoint and DHCP may leave; ports 53/853 outside the tunnel are dropped (DNS-leak guard). NMBV_KS_LAN=1 also allows LAN ranges.
.TP
.B nmbv run NAME cmd...
Runs cmd in its own network namespace whose only route is the tunnel, with a tunnel-only resolv.conf.
.TP
.B nmbv watch NAME | rtt HOST | probe HOST
Reconnect watchdog; ICMP round trip; path-MTU search (IPv4 only).
.TP
.B nmbv providers | relays P | connect P [--fastest] [--via COUNTRY]
Mullvad (WireGuard, local keypair, device registration, optional multihop) and NordVPN (NordLynx). Secrets are read from files named by NMBV_MULLVAD_ACCOUNT_FILE / NMBV_NORD_TOKEN_FILE (mode 0600) and passed to curl on stdin, never on argv. NMBV_MULLVAD_DNS selects the resolver.
.PP
Tested: everything WireGuard and OpenVPN (real handshake and data between two user+network namespaces, policy routing, DNS restore, kill switch, watchdog, run, rtt/MTU) and the provider JSON parsers against saved samples (nmb_vpn_test.c, 134 checks, ASan/UBSan clean). Not tested: the live Mullvad and NordVPN APIs (need accounts), Mullvad multihop, --fastest on a real network, PreUp/PostUp hooks in run mode.
